{"statement": "Shifting export controls from raw compute thresholds to algorithmic efficiency gains is not national security policy. It is a regulatory capture mechanism designed by incumbent frontier artificial intelligence laboratories to lock out independent academic competitors. The Bureau of Industry and Security currently relies on physical hardware limits such as floating-point operations per second to restrict access. Proponents claim that software optimizations make hardware metrics obsolete. They argue that efficiency breakthroughs bypass physical chip counts entirely. But this technical narrative ignores the institutional reality of regulatory compliance. Complex algorithmic auditing standards function as legal moats. They mirror the FDA approval processes that pharmaceutical giants use to crush generic competition before it reaches the market. Smaller labs and open-source projects cannot afford the continuous compliance overhead required to certify proprietary weight updates under a shifting efficiency regime.",
\n
Behind the technocratic debate over efficiency metrics lies an immense physical footprint that regulators consistently ignore. Foundation model training centers require staggering amounts of power and water. In Loudoun County, Virginia, data center clusters already consume over five hundred megawatts of electricity, straining local municipal grids. When the Department of Commerce focuses on abstract algorithmic efficiency instead of physical infrastructure, it sanitizes these concrete environmental and labor costs. Frontier laboratories pitch their training runs as weight-space geometry problems. On the ground, those runs demand diesel generators, cooling towers, and low-wage content moderators working under severe psychological distress. The state department administrative apparatus subsidizes these externalized costs by treating training efficiency as an esoteric security problem rather than an industrial zoning failure.",
\n
Opponents argue that hardware thresholds fail because clever engineers can train better models with fewer chips. This objection misunderstands the enforcement mechanism of trade administration. Raw compute caps on advanced semiconductors like Nvidia H100 units remain enforceable through customs inspections and supply chain auditing of silicon shipments. Algorithmic efficiency thresholds require the Bureau of Industry and Security to police intangible software math inside private corporate repositories. That enforcement mandate hands regulatory authority directly to the firms being regulated. Incumbent AI developers will write the compliance standards that certify their own efficiency gains. History shows that when the state delegates standard-setting to dominant monopolies, the result is always cartelization disguised as public safety. Policymakers must reject the efficiency shift and keep export controls anchored to physical hardware limits.",
\n
